\chapter{S-expressions, E-graphs and Equality Saturation}

This chapter builds the workflow from rule text to equality-triggered rewriting. Its APIs exist in \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:} in C++ and at module scope in D.

\section{1. Construct and read the interchange language}

An \texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} has \texttt{i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m}, \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t}, \texttt{i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}s}, and \texttt{q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d} fields. Atoms carry text; lists carry expressions. Prefer the \texttt{a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m}/\allowbreak{}\texttt{l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} factories, which establish the appropriate representation and quoting. D expressions deeply copy their mutable list storage; C++ expressions have ordinary vector value semantics.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto expression = dsl::SExpr::list({
    dsl::SExpr::atom("add"), dsl::SExpr::atom("a"), dsl::SExpr::atom("0")});
assert(expression.dump() == "(add a 0)");
auto parsed = dsl::parse_sexpr("; comment\n(add a 0)");
assert(parsed == expression);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto expression = SExpr.list([SExpr.atom("add"), SExpr.atom("a"), SExpr.atom("0")]);
assert(expression.dump() == "(add a 0)");
assert(parse_sexpr("; comment\n(add a 0)") == expression);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The notation is:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{ebnf}{}
\begin{MDCode}
expression = atom | quoted_atom | "(" , { expression } , ")" ;
quoted_atom = '"' , { character | escape } , '"' ;
escape = "\\n" | "\\r" | "\\t" | '\\"' | "\\\\" ;
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Whitespace separates unquoted atoms; parentheses delimit lists; a semicolon starts a comment through the end of the line. Empty lists and quoted empty atoms are valid S-expressions. Quotedness is retained. \texttt{d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} quotes atoms as necessary and escapes special characters. The reader bounds recursive nesting at 512 levels and rejects malformed lists/\allowbreak{}escapes.

\texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} requires exactly one expression. \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s} returns all expressions in a source, including an empty sequence for empty input.

\section{2. Understand tree conversion}

\texttt{a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} maps a leaf to \texttt{(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})} and an inner node to \texttt{(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})}. \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} maps a list with one atom argument to a leaf, other headed lists to inner nodes, and a bare atom to tag \texttt{a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m}. An operator head must be an atom. That convention cannot distinguish an inner node with one bare-atom child from a leaf payload; retain \texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} when the interchange model itself is the desired syntax tree.

An e-graph accepts an \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} through \texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})} and produces one through \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{})} using these conventions.

\section{3. Declare equality rules}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{lisp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
(rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
(= (add ?left ?right) (add ?right ?left))
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} reads one rule; \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} reads a sequence. Each \texttt{E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} stores \texttt{n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{l\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s}, and \texttt{r\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s}. Shorthand \texttt{=} and \texttt{=\allowbreak{}>} rules use the operator text as their name.

An unquoted atom starting with \texttt{?} and at least one following character is a variable. Quoted \texttt{"\allowbreak{}?\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}"} is a literal. Variables may occupy complete term positions, including the entire LHS, but operator heads are literal atoms. Every RHS variable must occur in the LHS. Empty lists and list-valued heads are invalid e-graph terms even though they are valid general S-expressions.

A rule's search orientation is LHS to RHS. Applying it records equality in both directions. Supply a reverse-oriented rule when that reverse pattern should independently trigger a search.

\section{4. Add terms and establish equality}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
dsl::EGraph graph;
auto a = graph.add("a");
auto b = graph.add("b");
auto fa = graph.add("(f a)");
auto fb = graph.add("(f b)");
assert(!graph.equivalent(fa, fb));
graph.unite(a, b);
assert(graph.equivalent(fa, fb));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Use \texttt{n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h} in D; the remaining calls have the same spelling.

An e-node records an operator, atom/\allowbreak{}quoted status, and child e-class IDs. Hash-consing shares nodes with the same canonical children. Union-find stores equivalence classes; \texttt{u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e} marks the graph for congruence rebuilding. \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}d} discovers parent equivalences and removes duplicate canonical nodes. Queries such as \texttt{e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t}, \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}, counts, and extraction rebuild first. \texttt{f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d} returns an ID's current representative. IDs stay valid after merging.

\texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} counts retained unique e-nodes, whereas \texttt{c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} counts equivalence classes. A class can retain several nodes/\allowbreak{}alternatives.

\section{5. Match by equality}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
graph.add("(pair a b)");
auto found = graph.matches(dsl::parse_sexpr("(pair ?x ?x)"));
assert(found.size() == 1); // a and b were merged above
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto graph = new EGraph;
auto a = graph.add("a"), b = graph.add("b");
graph.unite(a, b);
graph.add("(pair a b)");
auto found = graph.matches(parse_sexpr("(pair ?x ?x)"));
assert(found.length == 1);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{E\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t} identifies the matched class; \texttt{b\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}s} maps variable names to IDs. Matching explores alternatives within classes. Repeated variables compare canonical class IDs, so equality is sufficient even when textual leaves differ. \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} bounds the number of returned records.

\section{6. Saturate without discarding alternatives}

\texttt{s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{})} performs iterations of e-matching, RHS instantiation, equality merging and rebuilding. It reaches saturation when an iteration produces no graph revision. New RHS forms remain available to later rules and to later iterations. The original forms remain represented too.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
dsl::EGraph graph;
auto root = graph.add("(mul (add a 0) 1)");
auto report = graph.saturate(dsl::sexpr_rules(R"(
    (rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
    (rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
)"));
assert(report.saturated());
assert(graph.extract(root).dump() == "a");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{S\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s} fields/\allowbreak{}defaults are:

\begin{xltabular}{\linewidth}{|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X|>{\raggedleft\arraybackslash}X|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X|}
\hline
\textbf{Field} & \textbf{Default} & \textbf{Bound} \\ \hline
\texttt{i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s} & 30 & Number of saturation iterations \\ \hline
\texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} & 100000 & Retained graph node budget during insertion \\ \hline
\texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} & 100000 & Total successful rule applications \\ \hline
\end{xltabular}

\texttt{S\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t} exposes \texttt{i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p}, and \texttt{s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p} is \texttt{S\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d}, \texttt{I\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t}, \texttt{N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t}, or \texttt{M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t}. Partial results remain valid after a bounded stop; an interrupted RHS insertion can leave newly inserted subterms without merging its unfinished parent. The temporary node budget is restored when the run returns.

\section{7. Trigger user rewriters}

\texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} performs an explicit equality check and calls the supplied rewriter with the graph and canonical representative:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto atom = graph.add("a");
graph.when_equal(root, atom, [](dsl::EGraph& g, dsl::EClassId id) {
    auto tree = g.extract_ast(id);
    // Apply domain-specific AST transformations or visitor operations here.
    assert(tree.tag() == "atom");
});
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto atom = graph.add("a");
graph.when_equal(root, atom, (EGraph g, EClassId id) {
    auto tree = g.extract_ast(id);
    assert(tree.tag() == "atom");
});
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The return value says whether the callback ran. Each call checks current equality; this is a direct invocation API. For observing saturation itself, its third argument receives \texttt{(\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{})} after each completed application. An application may report an already-established equality, and a binding may be observed again in subsequent iterations. Keep observer accounting separate from the graph-change criterion for saturation.

\section{8. Extract a finite representative}

\texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{})} computes minimum node counts over the class alternatives, then constructs an \texttt{S\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}. Cyclic alternatives do not force a cyclic output when a finite representative exists. For example, merging \texttt{a} with \texttt{(\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{})} still permits extracting \texttt{a}. There is no user-supplied extraction cost model in the current API; the implemented metric is node count.

The integration examples linked from Chapter 30 combine rule preprocessing, saturation, equality-triggered visitors and grammar recognition. Chapters 34 and 35 explain the visitor and preprocessing stages in detail.
